sum='The speaker holds a round magnifying glass and says it can make “magic talk”. Through it, a tiny piece of chalk shows countless tiny shells, a small patch of moss looks like a whole forest with flowers and trees, and a drop of water seems as busy as a hive of bees. He waits and watches a skilful spider shoot silk from its spinnerets to weave its web; its claws look fierce like a tiger’s, and the foolish flies with their many eyes still blunder into the net. Nothing is so small that the glass cannot make it more marvellous. Finally he imagines that with lenses like this (telescopes) the moon would look so near that one could walk there in an afternoon. The poem has six four-line stanzas with the rhyme scheme ABCB and is full of wonder and curiosity.',
